Welp
To practice what you’ve learned about joining multiple tables, you are going to use data from an exciting app called Welp. Users love Welp because it allows them to leave reviews of businesses in their city and see how other people reviewed the businesses.

For this project, you will be working with two tables:

places
reviews

#Task 5 Seting up the tables and collummns:

select places.name, places.average_rating, reviews.username, reviews.rating, reviews.review_date, reviews.note
from places
inner join reviews
  on places.id = reviews.place_id;

#Task 6 Query with a left join:

select places.name, places.average_rating, reviews.username, reviews.rating, reviews.review_date, reviews.note
from places
left join reviews
  on places.id = reviews.place_id;

#Task 7 query to find all the id of places that currently do not have any reviews in our reviews table:

select places.id, places.name
from places
left join reviews
  on places.id = reviews.place_id
where reviews.place_id is null;

#Task 8 write a query using with clause to select all the reviews that happenend in 2020. join th palces to your with query to see a log of all reviews from 2020:

with reviews_2020 as (
  select *
  from reviews
  where strftime("%Y" , review_date) = "2020"
)
select *
from places
inner join reviews_2020
  on places.id = reviews_2020.place_id;

#Task 9 a query that finds the reviewer with the most reviews that are below the average rating for places:

with avg_rating as(
  select avg(average_rating) as avg
  from places
)
select reviews.username, count(*) as num_reviews
from places
inner join reviews
  on places.id = reviews.place_id
cross join avg_rating
where places.average_rating <= avg_rating.avg
group by reviews.username
order by num_reviews desc
limit 5;


